3. Simulate a Zomato food order journey by writing the steps/data packets that travel from your device to Zomato's server and back, mentioning protocols like HTTP, TCP/IP, and DNS wherever relevant.<br><br><em><strong>Hint:</strong> Think about what happens when you search for a restaurant and place an order.</em>
=>DNS Lookup
Your phone asks: “What is the IP address of www.zomato.com (or the Zomato app API)?”
→ Uses DNS protocol → receives Zomato’s public IP (or CDN IP).
TCP Connection
Phone starts a reliable connection to Zomato’s server using TCP (usually on port 443 for HTTPS).
→ TCP three-way handshake (SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK).
HTTPS / HTTP Request – Search
You type a restaurant name. The app sends an encrypted HTTPS (HTTP over TLS) request containing the search query.
→ Data travels: Phone → Wi-Fi Router → ISP → Internet → Zomato servers.
→ Zomato replies with a list of restaurants (JSON data) over the same HTTPS connection.
HTTPS Request – Place Order
You select items and tap “Place Order”.
→ Another HTTPS request is sent with order details, delivery address, payment info (tokenized), etc.
→ Protocols involved: TCP/IP (transport + network) + HTTPS (application).
Server Processing & Response
Zomato’s servers process the order, talk to payment gateways and the restaurant, then send back a confirmation (order ID, ETA, etc.) over the same HTTPS connection.
Return Path
The reply packets travel back: Zomato servers → Internet → ISP → your router → your phone.
The Zomato app displays the confirmation.

Key protocols at each layer:

DNS – Name → IP resolution
TCP – Reliable, ordered delivery of data
IP – Addressing and routing across networks
HTTPS (HTTP + TLS) – Encrypted application data (search queries, order details, payment tokens)

